---
import KnowledgeLayout from '../../layouts/KnowledgeLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/seo/FAQ.astro';
import FlowDiagram from '../../components/seo/FlowDiagram.astro';
import { countImage } from '../../engines/vision/count';
import { getModel } from '../../data/models';
const toc=[{id:'what',label:'What a dot is'},{id:'capable',label:'Why it is more capable'},{id:'where',label:'Where the tokens come from'},{id:'chat',label:'Agent against chat'},{id:'numbers',label:'The arithmetic'},{id:'published',label:'What is published'},{id:'faq',label:'Questions'}];

// Every figure is either a published rate from the catalogue or computed here from one,
// so the article cannot drift away from the pricing data.
const astra=getModel('gpt-6-astra');
const SCREENSHOT={width:1920,height:1080};
const screenshot=countImage(SCREENSHOT,astra).tokens;
const CHAT_IN=1_000, CHAT_OUT=500;              // the site's own "short chat turn" scenario
const TASK_OUT=27_000;                          // Artificial Analysis, per Intelligence Index task
const chatCost=(CHAT_IN/1e6)*astra.inputPerMillion+(CHAT_OUT/1e6)*astra.outputPerMillion;
const taskOutputCost=(TASK_OUT/1e6)*astra.outputPerMillion;
const screenshotCost=(screenshot/1e6)*astra.inputPerMillion;
const usd=(value:number)=>`$${value.toFixed(value<0.01?4:2)}`;
const faq=[
  {question:'What is OpenAI Dots?',answer:'Dots are always-on agents inside ChatGPT. A dot keeps a name, a job and memory that builds over time, runs on its own cloud computer with a browser, and keeps working after you close the tab. It is a product, not an API or a model id.'},
  {question:'What model powers Dots?',answer:'GPT-6 Astra. There is no separate Dots model and no Dots API. A developer who wants the same engine calls gpt-6-astra, at $10 per million input tokens and $50 per million output tokens.'},
  {question:'Is there a Dots API?',answer:'No. Dots is a ChatGPT product. The underlying model, GPT-6 Astra, is available through the normal API, but the agent, its memory and its hosted computer are not.'},
  {question:'Why would an agent use more tokens than a chat?',answer:'Three reasons that have nothing to do with being smarter. It wakes up on a schedule instead of waiting for you, so it runs when nobody asked. It re-sends its accumulated memory as input on every run. And work on a hosted computer produces screenshots, which are billed as image tokens.'},
  {question:'How many tokens is one screenshot?',answer:`A 1920x1080 screenshot is ${screenshot.toLocaleString()} tokens on GPT-6 Astra, using OpenAI's published 32x32 patch formula. That is more than twice a typical 1,000-token chat prompt.`},
];
const faqSchema={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage',mainEntity:faq.map((item)=>({'@type':'Question',name:item.question,acceptedAnswer:{'@type':'Answer',text:item.answer}}))};
---
<KnowledgeLayout title="OpenAI Dots: What an Always-On Agent Changes" description="Dots are GPT-6 Astra agents that keep working after you close the tab. What they can do, how they differ from a chat model, and why the token footprint changes." heading="OpenAI Dots: what an always-on agent changes" eyebrow="Agent products · Technical explainer" datePublished="2026-10-02" dateModified="2026-10-02" section="AI agents" keywords={['OpenAI Dots','always-on agents','Dots pricing','GPT-6 Astra','agent token usage']} {toc}>
  <p class="knowledge-lead">On 29 September 2026, at DevDay, OpenAI put an agent inside ChatGPT that keeps working after you close the tab. The capability jump is real. The part that surprises people arrives later, on the bill: a chat model runs when you press enter, and a dot runs when it decides to.</p>

  <h2 id="what">What a dot is</h2>
  <p>A dot is a named, persistent agent. It holds a goal over time, carries context between conversations, and brings finished work back for review. Five things define it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It has its own computer.</strong> Each dot runs on an isolated cloud machine with a browser, so the work continues when your laptop is shut.</li>
    <li><strong>It has memory that compounds.</strong> A dot is not a fresh session each time. What it learned yesterday is still there today.</li>
    <li><strong>It reaches your tools.</strong> More than 4,000 apps connect through the plugin ecosystem.</li>
    <li><strong>It lives where you work.</strong> ChatGPT on desktop, web and mobile, plus Slack and Teams.</li>
    <li><strong>It asks before acting.</strong> Dots follow ChatGPT's permission model, and you can set rules for when a dot may act alone and when it must stop and ask.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One dot is included with eligible paid plans: Pro and Business Premium, with Enterprise workspaces able to enable it on approval. OpenAI publishes no price for additional dots.</p>

  <h2 id="capable">Why it is more capable than a chat window</h2>
  <p>A chat model is a very good function: text in, text out, one turn at a time. Everything else in a chat product—files, memory, tools—is scaffolding around that function. A dot moves three of those from scaffolding into the product itself.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Time.</strong> A chat model has no concept of later. A dot does: it can check something tomorrow morning and tell you what changed.</li>
    <li><strong>A computer.</strong> Reasoning about a website is weaker than opening it. A hosted browser lets a dot read the page, click through it and come back with the answer instead of a guess.</li>
    <li><strong>Accountability.</strong> Named agents with their own permissions mean work can be delegated to a specific dot with a specific scope, rather than to one assistant that sees everything.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>That is the intelligence story, and it is genuinely new. The cost story follows directly from it.</p>

