  <h2 id="chat">An agent is not a chat, in four ways</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>It runs without you.</strong> Scheduled and proactive work happens on the dot's clock. Nobody presses enter, and nobody sees the cost until the invoice.</li>
    <li><strong>Memory is input.</strong> Persistent context is not free storage. It is re-sent as input tokens on every run, and it grows over the life of the dot. Caching reduces the rate—OpenAI charges {usd(astra.cachedInputPerMillion!)} per million for cached input against {usd(astra.inputPerMillion)}—but it does not remove the volume.</li>
    <li><strong>Screens are images.</strong> Computer use means screenshots. A 1920 × 1080 screenshot is <strong>{screenshot.toLocaleString()} input tokens</strong> on GPT-6 Astra under OpenAI's published patch formula. Twenty screenshots in one session is {(screenshot*20).toLocaleString()} tokens, before the dot writes a word.</li>
    <li><strong>Thinking is billed as output.</strong> Reasoning tokens are output tokens, at {usd(astra.outputPerMillion)} per million. A long deliberation costs the same as a long answer.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="numbers">The arithmetic, in chat turns</h2>
  <p>A short chat turn is about {CHAT_IN.toLocaleString()} input and {CHAT_OUT} output tokens. On GPT-6 Astra that is {usd(chatCost)}.</p>
  <p>For agent work there is a published measurement to anchor on. Artificial Analysis, reported by VentureBeat, measured GPT-6 Astra Max at roughly <strong>{TASK_OUT.toLocaleString()} output tokens per task</strong> on its Intelligence Index—the lowest of the frontier agents it measured, and still {Math.round(TASK_OUT/CHAT_OUT)} times the output of one chat turn. At the published rate, that output alone is {usd(taskOutputCost)}.</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Unit of work</th><th>Tokens</th><th>On GPT-6 Astra</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>One short chat turn</td><td>{CHAT_IN.toLocaleString()} in + {CHAT_OUT} out</td><td>{usd(chatCost)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One screenshot of a 1080p screen</td><td>{screenshot.toLocaleString()} in</td><td>{usd(screenshotCost)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One measured agent task, output only</td><td>{TASK_OUT.toLocaleString()} out</td><td>{usd(taskOutputCost)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The same task, once an hour for a working day</td><td>{(TASK_OUT*8).toLocaleString()} out</td><td>{usd(taskOutputCost*8)}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The last row is the point of this article. Nothing in it assumes the agent is wasteful. It assumes only that the agent does what it was built to do: run on a schedule. Eight runs of one task is {Math.round(TASK_OUT*8/CHAT_OUT)} chat turns of output, produced by one agent, on one job, in one day.</p>
  <p>This is why a per-seat subscription is the natural way to sell an agent and an awkward way to budget for one. The subscription is fixed; the work behind it is not.</p>

  <h2 id="published">What OpenAI publishes, and what it does not</h2>
  <p><strong>Published:</strong> the GPT-6 Astra API rates used throughout this page ({usd(astra.inputPerMillion)} input, {usd(astra.cachedInputPerMillion!)} cached input and {usd(astra.outputPerMillion)} output per million, doubling above 272K input tokens); that one dot is included with eligible paid plans; that proactive background research is read-only, so a dot cannot send messages or change app content on its own.</p>
  <p><strong>Not published:</strong> any token allowance attached to a dot, the price of additional dots, and how much of a plan's usage a dot consumes. Until those appear, the honest answer to "what does a dot cost per month" is the plan price, and the honest answer to "what does the work cost" is the API arithmetic above.</p>

